"{\"blocks\":[{\"k\":\"t\",\"v\":\"If you’re open to positioning and want a business-cabin award, investigate this option first: it offers a clear cabin advantage over the cash benchmark, but depends on separate tickets and leaves key costs and availability unconfirmed.\"},{\"k\":\"p\",\"r\":\"J63@2027-01-08T08:36-08:00/SFO>BKK/B>2027-01-10T04:15\",\"n\":\"Business award via LAX\",\"v\":\"The award itinerary continues from LAX via Istanbul to Bangkok, so this is not a nonstop trip. The SFO–LAX positioning flight arrives 150 minutes before the award departure, but the tickets are separate and protection is unknown. The award is listed at 275,000 Turkish miles; fees, traveler support, and price scope are unknown, and the full price is incomplete. Leg-by-leg cabins are also unconfirmed.\"},{\"k\":\"b\",\"r\":\"C1@2027-01-08T06:05-08:00/SFO>BKK\",\"n\":\"Cash benchmark\",\"v\":\"The returned cash itinerary is $452 USD, with two stops via Seattle and Seoul and a Bangkok arrival at 00:20—earlier than the award option’s 04:15 arrival. Its quote scope is unknown, so treat it only as a cash baseline, not a per-person or all-in comparison.\"},{\"k\":\"t\",\"v\":\"For the award option, the known SFO–LAX cash component is $59 USD, also with unknown quote scope; it does not include a known cash price for the award, whose fees are unknown. The search returned one traveler for the cash records, but that does not establish the award’s traveler support or quote scope. Confirm award availability, current pricing, and the consequences of separate tickets before pursuing it. Results may not be real-time, and the source coverage was partial.\"}]}"
